<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giuseppe Verdi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Verdi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giuseppe Verdi was an Italian opera composer, born at Le Roncole near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/busseto">Busseto</a> in the Duchy of Parma. His operas, among them Nabucco, Rigoletto, La traviata, Il trovatore, Aida, Otello and Falstaff, made him the dominant figure of nineteenth-century Italian opera. Works such as I vespri siciliani and his public support for unification made him a symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Risorgimento</a>, and he sat in the first Italian parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi.jpg" alt="Giuseppe Verdi" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ferdinand Mulnier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giuseppe_Verdi_by_Ferdinand_Mulnier_BW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Verdi was the first child of Carlo Giuseppe Verdi and Luigia Uttini, an innkeeper and a spinner according to the baptismal register of 11 October 1813. He was born at Le Roncole, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/busseto">Busseto</a>, then in the Taro department of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-french-empire">First French Empire</a>, and from the age of four had private lessons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Italian. He learned the organ and showed so much interest in music that his parents gave him a spinet. With the help of a local patron, Antonio Barezzi, he received a musical education. In 1835 he became director of the Busseto school, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-scala">La Scala</a> impresario Bartolomeo Merelli agreed to stage his first opera, Oberto, in November 1839.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Nabucco, in 1842, made his reputation, and I Lombardi alla prima crociata followed in 1843. Verdi then wrote twenty operas over sixteen years, a period he later called sixteen years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>. Rigoletto, based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>'s Le roi s'amuse, was written for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1851, and with Il trovatore and La traviata it cemented his fame. Censors objected to Stiffelio and Rigoletto, and Verdi refused to compromise on the hunchbacked hero of the latter.</p><p>After La traviata he wrote only six new stage works in eighteen years, up to Aida, and he returned happily to his estate at Sant'Agata. In 1859 he began to take an active interest in Italian politics, and he served briefly as an elected politician. After the success of Otello in 1887 he said that having massacred so many heroes and heroines he had the right to laugh a little, and with a libretto by Boito he wrote the comedy Falstaff, of 1893.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Critics divide Verdi's work into early, middle and late periods. Macbeth of 1847 has been called his first masterpiece, and his late masterpieces are the Requiem of 1874 and the operas Otello and Falstaff. He was influenced by Rossini, Bellini and Donizetti and by the French Grand opera of Meyerbeer and Spontini.</p><p>Historians debate how political his operas were. The chorus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero</a> from Nabucco became an anthem of Italian patriots, and from 1859 the slogan Viva VERDI was used as an acronym for Viva Vittorio Emanuele Re D'Italia. The music historian Philip Gossett notes that myths exaggerating his early nationalism circulated in the nineteenth century: the audience is said to have demanded an encore of Va, pensiero at its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> premiere, but the piece encored was in fact the hymn Immenso Jehova.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giuseppe Verdi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Giuseppe Verdi</b> was born in the duchy’s territory, at Le Roncole near Busseto, in 1813.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Culturally the period produced the operas of <b>Giuseppe Verdi</b> and Giacomo Puccini, the Futurist movement, Guglielmo Marconi’s radio and the educational method of Maria Montessori, while the racial laws of 1938 drove scientists such as Enrico Fermi into exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Vespers of 1282 became a symbol of popular resistance and inspired <b>Giuseppe Verdi</b>’s opera, and the division they caused gave rise to two kingdoms both called Sicily and eventually to the name Two Sicilies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians disagree on how political <b>Verdi</b>'s operas were: Franco Della Peruta stresses his patriotic intent, while Roger Parker argues that nationalistic historians exaggerated it.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…from the reign of Khedive Ismail: it was played at the opening of the Suez Canal and adopted in 1871, an instrumental piece without official words whose most likely author was Giuseppe Pugioli, an Italian cornetist who had come to Egypt to perform <b>Verdi</b>'s opera Aida and stayed.</blockquote><a href="/songs/biladi-biladi-biladi">Biladi, Biladi, Biladi</a></li><li><blockquote>Garibaldi's volunteers sang it in the Expedition of the Thousand in 1860, and <b>Giuseppe Verdi</b> chose it to stand for Italy in his &quot;Hymn of the Nations&quot; for the London International Exhibition of 1862.</blockquote><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Le Roncole, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.95,10.07&amp;year=1870" aria-label="Le Roncole on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Giuseppe Verdi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5606 3709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5606 3709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.95,10.07&amp;year=1870">What was here in 1870? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
